<h2 id="training-controllers-simple-imu-stabilization">Training controllers: simple IMU stabilization</h2>
<p>Flight controllers are small, single-board computers that assist with controlling an aircraft. They typically use an <strong>IMU (Inertial Measurement Unit)</strong> containing sensors such as accelerometers and gyroscopes to measure the aircraft&#x27;s motion and orientation. More advanced systems may also use a barometer, magnetometer, GPS receiver, airspeed sensor, and other sensors to determine altitude, heading, position, speed, and other flight information.</p>
<p>One of the simplest uses of a flight controller is to help a new pilot learn to fly. A basic stabilization system can automatically compensate for disturbances, limit the amount of pitch and roll commanded by the pilot, and, in some modes, return the aircraft toward level flight when the pilot releases the controls. Some systems can also limit the aircraft&#x27;s attitude to help prevent a beginning pilot from accidentally rolling the airplane inverted.</p>
<p>These controllers vary considerably in features and cost, but stabilization can be a useful training aid, particularly for someone learning to fly without an experienced RC pilot providing instruction.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/training-stabilizer.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/training-stabilizer.jpg" alt="A small red circuit board labeled Aura Lite, with a square microcontroller chip, two blue buttons labeled BIND, rows of three-pin servo headers, a micro-USB port, and two white connectors." width="1130" height="1073" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A small training stabilizer: a microcontroller and motion sensors on one board, connected between the receiver and the servos.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="pixhawk-and-other-open-source-flight-controllers">Pixhawk and other open-source flight controllers</h2>
<p>More advanced flight controllers can provide stabilization as well as <strong>autonomous flight</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Pixhawk</strong> refers to an open flight-controller hardware ecosystem and associated hardware standards. Pixhawk-compatible controllers are commonly used with open-source autopilot software such as <strong>ArduPilot</strong> or <strong>PX4</strong>. These projects are extensively documented and supported, so this section will focus on the basic concepts rather than every configuration option.</p>
<p>A Pixhawk-compatible flight controller is a small computer built around one or more microcontrollers and a collection of sensors. Depending on the software installed, these controllers can operate many different types of vehicles, including:</p>
<ul><li>
Fixed-wing airplanes
</li><li>
Multirotors
</li><li>
Helicopters
</li><li>
VTOL aircraft
</li><li>
Ground vehicles
</li><li>
Boats
</li><li>
Submersibles
</li></ul>
<p>The flight controller receives information from its sensors, the pilot&#x27;s receiver, and other connected devices. It processes this information and sends commands to servos, electronic speed controllers, motors, and other aircraft systems.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/pixhawk-2-4-8.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/pixhawk-2-4-8.jpg" alt="A black Pixhawk 2.4.8 flight controller case. Labeled ports include GPS, two telemetry ports, I2C, CAN, power, USB, SPI, and a switch, and an arrow on top points toward the front of the aircraft." width="700" height="409" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A Pixhawk-compatible flight controller (Pixhawk 2.4.8). The labeled ports connect GPS, telemetry radios, power, and other devices; the arrow points toward the front of the aircraft.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>SpeedyBee</strong> manufactures compact flight controllers commonly used in multirotor aircraft. Some models can be stacked directly with an ESC board, creating a compact and clean installation.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/flight-controller.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/flight-controller.jpg" alt="Top view of a SpeedyBee F405 V4 flight controller: a square black circuit board with a mounting hole in each corner, a processor chip in the middle, a USB-C port on the right, and rows of labeled solder pads along the top and bottom edges." width="1280" height="1280" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A flight controller (SpeedyBee F405 V4).</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="flight-control-software">Flight-control software</h2>
<p>A flight controller requires software to operate. The software stored and executed by the flight controller&#x27;s microcontroller is called <strong>firmware</strong>.</p>
<p>Several open-source projects are commonly encountered in unmanned aircraft.</p>
<h3 id="betaflight">Betaflight</h3>
<p>Betaflight is open-source flight-control firmware commonly associated with multirotors, particularly FPV aircraft. It provides stabilization, configuration, flight modes, and extensive tuning capabilities.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/betaflight-configurator.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/betaflight-configurator.jpg" alt="The Betaflight Configurator 10.10.0 welcome screen, with Update Firmware and Connect buttons at the top right and a menu on the left: Welcome, Privacy Policy, Documentation &amp; Support, Options, and Firmware Flasher." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Betaflight Configurator, the computer program used to set up a flight controller running Betaflight.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="ardupilot-and-mission-planner">ArduPilot and Mission Planner</h3>
<p><strong>ArduPilot</strong> is open-source autopilot firmware capable of controlling airplanes, multirotors, helicopters, rovers, boats, and other vehicles.</p>
<p><strong>Mission Planner</strong> is ground-control-station software commonly used with ArduPilot. It allows the user to configure the aircraft, view telemetry, calibrate sensors, select parameters, and create autonomous missions.</p>
<p><a href="https://ardupilot.org/planner/">Download Mission Planner from ArduPilot</a> (Windows). It works with the ASCEND flight controller as it is, and so does <a href="/software/#software-rc">QGroundControl</a>, which also runs on Mac and Linux. Use whichever your team prefers. Whichever you use, update the ASCEND flight controller’s firmware only with the <a href="/resources/transmitter-configurator/">RC Configurator</a>, never from the ground control station; see the <a href="/resources/flight-controller-v4-setup/">V4 setup guide</a>.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/mission-planner.png"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/mission-planner.png" alt="Mission Planner’s Flight Data screen. On the left, an artificial horizon reads DISARMED with a pre-arm warning, above readouts for altitude, ground speed, distance to waypoint, yaw, vertical speed, and distance to the ground station. On the right is a satellite map. Tabs across the top include Flight Data, Flight Plan, Initial Setup, Config/Tuning, and Simulation." width="1105" height="671" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Mission Planner’s Flight Data screen shows the aircraft’s attitude, telemetry, and position on a map. Missions are built on the Flight Plan tab.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h3 id="qgroundcontrol">QGroundControl</h3>
<p><strong>QGroundControl</strong> is another ground-control-station application used to configure supported autopilot systems, monitor aircraft, and plan autonomous missions.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgroundcontrol.png"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgroundcontrol.png" alt="QGroundControl over a satellite map of a park. A takeoff point and six numbered waypoints are joined by an orange route, a prompt reads “Takeoff from ground and start the current mission,” and instruments, a compass, and a video window surround the map." width="1005" height="600" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>QGroundControl with a planned mission: a takeoff point and six waypoints, ready to start.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="automating-a-mission">Automating a mission</h2>
<p>One of the capabilities that distinguishes many UAS from traditional manually controlled RC aircraft is the ability to perform <strong>autonomous or automated flight</strong>.</p>
<p>Airplanes, helicopters, multirotors, VTOL aircraft, and other vehicles can all use automation. Depending on the aircraft and flight-control system, automation can include:</p>
<ul><li>
Automatic takeoff
</li><li>
Automatic landing
</li><li>
Flying a preprogrammed route using GPS waypoints
</li><li>
Taking photographs at predetermined locations, times, or distances
</li><li>
Loitering or holding near a GPS location
</li><li>
Surveying or mapping an area
</li><li>
Releasing a payload
</li><li>
Returning automatically to a launch or home location
</li></ul>
<p>A basic autonomous system generally requires an <strong>aircraft, flight controller, appropriate sensors, a control link, and software for configuring and programming the flight controller</strong>. GPS is required for most position-based autonomous operations.</p>
<p>Adding automation also introduces additional risks. Understanding those risks and knowing how to respond when automation does not behave as expected are important parts of operating an autonomous aircraft.</p>
<h3 id="example-plan-a-mission-in-qgroundcontrol">Example: plan a mission in QGroundControl</h3>
<p>This example walks through building a simple autonomous mission for the ASCEND airplane in QGroundControl. The mission has three parts:</p>
<ol>
<li>a <strong>takeoff</strong> that climbs to a safe altitude,</li>
<li>a loop of <strong>waypoints</strong> around the field, and</li>
<li>a <strong>loiter</strong> that circles until the pilot takes control and lands.</li>
</ol>
<p>That is the mission the <a href="/resources/mission-planning-lab/">Mission Planning lab</a> asks you to fly. The screenshots were taken in QGroundControl 5.1.4 on a Mac. The steps are the same on a PC. If you prefer to watch, the <a href="/resources/qgroundcontrol-mission-video/">QGroundControl mission video</a> covers the same mission.</p>
<p>You can plan the whole mission without the airplane connected. You only need the airplane to upload the finished plan.</p>
<h4 id="qgc-1-before-you-start">1. Before you start</h4>
<ul>
<li>Install <a href="/software/#software-rc">QGroundControl</a> on your laptop.</li>
<li>Set up the flight controller with the <a href="/resources/flight-controller-v4-setup/">ASCEND Flight Controller V4 setup guide</a>, including the calibrations and the failsafe test.</li>
<li>Know where you will fly. You need a field large enough for the whole route to stay within visual line of sight, clear of people, roads and buildings.</li>
<li>Read <a href="#safety-around-flight-controlled-aircraft">Safety around flight-controlled aircraft</a>, <a href="#failsafes">Failsafes</a>, <a href="#planning-a-mission">Planning a mission</a> and <a href="#flight-modes">Flight modes</a> later in this chapter.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="qgc-2-open-the-plan-view">2. Open the Plan view</h4>
<p>Click the <strong>Q</strong> icon in the top-left corner, then click <strong>Plan</strong>.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/01-plan-menu.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/01-plan-menu.jpg" alt="QGroundControl's main menu, opened from the Q icon in the top-left corner. Six tiles are shown: Fly, Plan, Configure, Analyze, Settings and Close. Plan is highlighted. The header reads Disconnected - Click to manually connect, and the version is 5.1.4." width="1200" height="562" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>The QGroundControl menu. Plan is where you build missions. You don't need to be connected to the airplane.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h4 id="qgc-3-check-the-plan-settings">3. Check the plan settings</h4>
<p>The Plan view shows a map with tools down the left side and the plan settings on the right. Scroll the map to your flying field.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/02-plan-view.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/02-plan-view.jpg" alt="The full Plan view. A satellite map of farm fields fills the screen with a green home marker near the top. Along the top are the Open, Save, Upload and Clear buttons. A tool column on the left lists Takeoff, Pattern, Waypoint, ROI and Land. The Plan Info panel is on the right, and an empty altitude graph runs along the bottom." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>The Plan view before any mission items are added. The green house icon is the expected home position. Select the image to open it full size.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>Check these settings in the panel on the right:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vehicle Info:</strong> <strong>ArduPilot</strong> and <strong>Fixed Wing</strong>. When the airplane is connected, QGroundControl reads these from the flight controller.</li>
<li><strong>Expected Home Position:</strong> drag the green home marker to your launch point. This is only for planning: the airplane sets its real home position from GPS when it arms.</li>
<li><strong>Defaults → Alt Frame:</strong> <strong>Rel</strong> (relative) means each altitude is measured from the home position, not from sea level.</li>
<li><strong>Defaults → Waypoints Altitude:</strong> the altitude every new waypoint starts with. Choose it for your local terrain and your mission objectives; the example uses <strong>75 ft</strong>. Set this before you add waypoints, so you don't have to change each one later.</li>
<li><strong>Flight Speed:</strong> leave it unchecked. The airplane then flies at its normal cruise speed.</li>
</ul>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/03-plan-settings.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/03-plan-settings.jpg" alt="Close-up of the Plan Info panel. Vehicle Info is set to ArduPilot and Fixed Wing. Under Expected Home Position: Drag to move home position, click to set new position, altitude 4891.7 feet above mean sea level, actual position set by vehicle at flight time. Plan Templates offers Survey, Corridor Scan and No Template. Under Defaults: Alt Frame Rel, Waypoints Altitude 75.0 feet on a 0 to 400 slider, and Flight Speed unchecked at 33.6 miles per hour." width="481" height="1533" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Plan settings used in this guide: ArduPilot, Fixed Wing, relative altitudes, and a default waypoint altitude of 75 ft.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>The 0–400 ft slider matches the FAA Part 107 limit of 400 ft above ground level. A relative altitude is measured from the home position. If the ground under your route rises or falls, the airplane's height above that ground changes too. The altitude graph at the bottom of the screen shows this (step 5).</p>
<h4 id="qgc-4-add-the-takeoff">4. Add the takeoff</h4>
<ol>
<li>Click <strong>Takeoff</strong> in the tool column on the left.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/04-takeoff-tool.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/04-takeoff-tool.jpg" alt="The top-left corner of the Plan view, with the Takeoff tool highlighted at the top of the tool column. Below it are Pattern, Waypoint, ROI and Land." width="1152" height="679" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>The Takeoff tool, at the top of the tool column.</p></figcaption></figure>
<ol start="2">
<li>A green <strong>T</strong> marker appears near home. Drag it to the point where the airplane should finish its climb-out. QGroundControl reminds you to keep that path clear of obstacles and to take off <strong>into the wind</strong>. Click <strong>Done</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/05-takeoff-placed.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/05-takeoff-placed.jpg" alt="A green circle labelled T Takeoff sits on the map just south of a road. In the panel on the right, the Takeoff item reads: Move T Takeoff to the climbout location. Ensure clear of obstacles and into the wind. A Done button is below." width="1400" height="867" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Place the takeoff marker on the climb-out path, then click Done.</p></figcaption></figure>
<ol start="3">
<li>Set the takeoff values:
<ul>
<li><strong>Altitude (Rel):</strong> the height the airplane climbs to before it heads for the first waypoint. The altitude is your team's choice, based on the local terrain and your mission objectives. The example uses 75 ft.</li>
<li><strong>Pitch:</strong> the minimum nose-up angle held during the climb. ASCEND teams use <strong>15°</strong>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/06-takeoff-settings.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/06-takeoff-settings.jpg" alt="The Takeoff item's settings in the right panel: Altitude (Rel) 75.0 feet on a 0 to 400 slider, and Pitch 15.00 degrees on a 0 to 45 slider. The T Takeoff marker is on the map to the left." width="1400" height="867" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Takeoff settings: climb to 75 ft relative altitude at a pitch of at least 15°.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>Launching for this example.</strong> Hand launch the airplane in MANUAL or FBWA and fly it up to a safe altitude yourself. Then flip the mode switch to AUTO (step 8) to start the mission. Keep the takeoff item in the plan anyway: it sets the altitude the mission climbs to before the first waypoint.</p>
<h4 id="qgc-5-add-the-waypoints">5. Add the waypoints</h4>
<ol>
<li>Click <strong>Waypoint</strong> in the tool column. It stays selected (highlighted) until you click it again.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/07-waypoint-tool.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/07-waypoint-tool.jpg" alt="The top-left corner of the Plan view with the Waypoint tool highlighted in the tool column. The Takeoff tool above it is now greyed out, and the Save button in the top bar is active." width="1152" height="679" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>With the Waypoint tool selected, every click on the map adds a waypoint.</p></figcaption></figure>
<ol start="2">
<li>Click the map where you want the first waypoint. QGroundControl numbers it <strong>2</strong>, because the takeoff is item 1. An orange line joins the takeoff to the waypoint, with an arrow showing the direction of flight.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/08-first-waypoint.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/08-first-waypoint.jpg" alt="An orange line runs south from the takeoff marker T to a green waypoint numbered 2. The Waypoint panel on the right shows Altitude (Rel) 75.0 feet and an unchecked Flight Speed. The graph at the bottom shows an orange line climbing from the home point to waypoint 2, above a flat green terrain line." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>The first waypoint. In the graph at the bottom, the orange line is the planned altitude and the green line is the ground.</p></figcaption></figure>
<ol start="3">
<li>Keep clicking to add more waypoints. The lab asks for <strong>at least four</strong>. Each one takes the default altitude from step 3. To change a single waypoint, select it and edit its <strong>Altitude (Rel)</strong> in the panel.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/09-waypoint-loop.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/09-waypoint-loop.jpg" alt="An orange four-sided loop on the map: from takeoff T south to waypoint 2, west to 3, north to 4, then east to waypoint 5 near the takeoff point. The altitude graph shows the climb to waypoint 2 and a level line at the same altitude through waypoints 3, 4 and 5." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Four waypoints (2 to 5) make a loop around the field and bring the airplane back near the launch point.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>A few things to know while placing waypoints:</p>
<ul>
<li>Drag a numbered marker to move that waypoint.</li>
<li>The small <strong>+</strong> circle halfway along each leg inserts a new waypoint there.</li>
<li>The trash-can icon in a waypoint's panel deletes it.</li>
<li>Keep the turns gentle. An airplane can't stop and turn on the spot, so a sharp corner makes it overshoot the waypoint and swing wide.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="qgc-6-end-the-mission-with-a-loiter">6. End the mission with a loiter</h4>
<p>A mission that simply ends leaves the airplane to decide what to do next. Instead, finish with a <strong>Loiter</strong>: the airplane circles one point until you take control. The lab finishes the same way: circle near the launch area, then switch to manual and land.</p>
<ol>
<li>With the Waypoint tool still selected, click inside the loop to add one last waypoint (6 in the example).</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/10-final-waypoint.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/10-final-waypoint.jpg" alt="A sixth waypoint, numbered 6, has been added inside the loop, joined to waypoint 5 by a diagonal orange line. The Mission Items list on the right shows Takeoff followed by four Waypoint entries, with the sixth item open in the panel." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A final waypoint placed inside the loop, near the launch area. Next, change it to a Loiter.</p></figcaption></figure>
<ol start="2">
<li>In the panel on the right, click the item's name, <strong>Waypoint ▾</strong>. The <strong>Select Mission Command</strong> window opens.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/11-mission-command.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/11-mission-command.jpg" alt="The Select Mission Command window, category Basic, listing Waypoint, Return To Launch, Land, Takeoff, Spline waypoint and Delay, each with a one-line description. On the right, the selected item's header reads Waypoint with a small drop-down arrow." width="1400" height="837" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Clicking the item's name (right) opens Select Mission Command, which can change it to a different kind of mission item.</p></figcaption></figure>
<ol start="3">
<li>Open the <strong>Category</strong> list and choose <strong>Loiter</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/12-command-category.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/12-command-category.jpg" alt="The Category drop-down open, listing Basic, Loiter, Flight control, Advanced, Conditionals, Camera, Safety and All commands." width="700" height="1060" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>The command categories. Loiter is second in the list.</p></figcaption></figure>
<ol start="4">
<li>Choose the first command, <strong>Loiter</strong>, which circles the point indefinitely. The other three stop after a number of turns, an amount of time, or when the airplane reaches an altitude.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/13-loiter-commands.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/13-loiter-commands.jpg" alt="The Loiter category with four commands: Loiter, travel to a position and loiter around it indefinitely; Loiter (turns), for a number of turns; Loiter (time), for an amount of time; and Loiter (altitude), until an altitude is reached." width="700" height="858" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Choose Loiter, the first command, to circle until the pilot takes over.</p></figcaption></figure>
<ol start="5">
<li>The marker becomes <strong>L</strong> and a circle shows the loiter path. Check the <strong>Altitude (Rel)</strong> and the <strong>Radius</strong>. The default radius is 164.04 ft (50 m). An arrow on the circle shows which way the airplane will circle.</li>
</ol>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/14-loiter-set.jpg"><img src="/assets/flight-controllers/qgc-mission/14-loiter-set.jpg" alt="The finished mission. The last item is now an L Loiter marker inside the loop, with an orange circle and arrows showing the loiter path. The Loiter panel on the right shows Altitude (Rel) 75.0 feet and Radius 164.04 feet." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>The finished mission: takeoff, four waypoints, and a 164 ft (50 m) loiter circle at 75 ft.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>The loiter radius and the circling direction are your team's choice.</p>
<h4 id="qgc-7-save-the-plan">7. Save the plan</h4>
<p>Click <strong>Save</strong> in the top bar and give the plan a name. QGroundControl saves it as a <code>.plan</code> file. Use <strong>Open</strong> to load it again later, on the same laptop or another one. Save before you go to the field, and keep a copy you can hand to your instructor.</p>
<h4 id="qgc-8-upload-and-review">8. Upload and review</h4>
<p><strong>Upload</strong> stays greyed out until the airplane is connected. At the field, with the propeller removed:</p>
<ol>
<li>Connect the airplane and wait for a 3D GPS fix. The real home position is set when the airplane arms.</li>
<li>Open the Plan view and click <strong>Upload</strong> to send the mission to the flight controller.</li>
<li>Check every item in the list once more: the order, each altitude, and the loiter at the end.</li>
<li>Make sure every waypoint is clear of trees, buildings and power lines at its planned altitude, and that the whole route stays where you can see the airplane.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Set up a mode switch for AUTO.</strong> Teams set their flight modes on channel 5, channel 6, or both. For this mission, use the channel 5 switch: set one position to <strong>AUTO</strong> and the other to <strong>MANUAL</strong> or <strong>FBWA</strong>. Once you have launched in MANUAL or FBWA and reached a safe altitude, flipping the switch to AUTO starts the mission. Flipping it back gives you control again at any point. Set the modes in QGroundControl under <strong>Vehicle Setup → Flight Modes</strong>. A two-position switch uses Flight Mode 1 (switch low) and Flight Mode 6 (switch high). With the propeller removed, flip the switch and check that the mode shown in the toolbar changes.</p>
<p class="notice"><strong>Before you fly</strong>Review the whole mission with your instructor. Practise flipping the mode switch out of AUTO, so you can take control in MANUAL or FBWA at any point in the mission. If the radio link is lost, the failsafe returns the airplane to launch at 100 m (about 330 ft). Follow the FAA rules that apply to your flight.</p>
<h4 id="qgc-fly-the-mission">Fly the mission</h4>
<p>Fly this mission in the <a href="/resources/mission-planning-lab/">Mission Planning lab</a>, as part of the <a href="/flight-controllers/flight-modes-and-missions/">Flight Modes and Mission Planning</a> unit. The <a href="https://docs.qgroundcontrol.com/master/en/qgc-user-guide/plan_view/plan_view.html">QGroundControl documentation</a> describes every tool in the Plan view.</p>
<h2 id="safety-around-flight-controlled-aircraft">Safety around flight-controlled aircraft</h2>
<p>An aircraft equipped with a flight controller may start its motors unexpectedly if it is armed or improperly configured. Changes in flight mode, failsafe conditions, programming errors, or unexpected system behavior can also cause motors or servos to activate.</p>
<p>Automation can fail because of <strong>mechanical, electrical, software, sensor, communication, or user errors</strong>. Flyaways, crashes, and injuries or damage to people and property are possible whenever an aircraft is operated.</p>
<p>Before operating an automated aircraft, you should understand:</p>
<ul><li>
How to arm and disarm the aircraft
</li><li>
How to stop the motors
</li><li>
How to switch between flight modes
</li><li>
How to regain manual control
</li><li>
What the aircraft will do if communication is lost
</li><li>
What its programmed failsafes will do
</li><li>
How to manually fly the aircraft if stabilization or automation becomes unavailable
</li></ul>
<p class="notice"><strong>Remove the propeller</strong>As a general safety practice, remove the propeller whenever you are configuring, programming, troubleshooting, or testing an electric aircraft indoors unless the propeller is specifically required for a controlled test and appropriate safety precautions are in place.</p>
<p>Maintain a safe operating area during flight and make sure everyone involved understands where they may safely stand. The pilot in command is responsible for conducting the flight safely.</p>
<h2 id="failsafes">Failsafes</h2>
<p>A <strong>failsafe</strong> is a predetermined response that occurs when the flight-control system detects a problem or specific condition. One common example is loss of the radio-control link between the aircraft and transmitter.</p>
<p>On a simple RC aircraft, a receiver failsafe might command the throttle to zero and place other channels into predetermined positions. Exactly what happens depends on how the receiver and aircraft have been configured.</p>
<p>A flight controller can provide much more sophisticated failsafe behavior. Depending on the aircraft, sensors, and configuration, a lost-link failsafe might cause the aircraft to:</p>
<ul><li>
Reduce or stop the motor
</li><li>
Maintain or stabilize its attitude
</li><li>
Loiter or circle while waiting for the radio link to return
</li><li>
Return to a programmed home location
</li><li>
Land automatically
</li><li>
Perform another predetermined action
</li></ul>
<p>If the aircraft has GPS and a valid home position, <strong>Return-to-Launch (RTL)</strong> may allow it to navigate back toward its launch location.</p>
<p>Automatically landing a multirotor is generally simpler than automatically landing a conventional fixed-wing airplane. A multirotor can descend nearly vertically into a relatively small area. A fixed-wing airplane must maintain forward airspeed and fly an approach to a suitable landing area. Wind direction, groundspeed, approach path, obstacles, and available landing distance can therefore become important considerations.</p>
<p>Failsafes must be properly configured and tested before relying on them.</p>
<h2 id="planning-a-mission">Planning a mission</h2>
<p>An autonomous mission can be created using ground-control-station software such as <strong>Mission Planner</strong>.</p>
<p>The operator can place GPS waypoints on a map and assign commands or parameters to different portions of the mission. Depending on the aircraft and software, these can include:</p>
<ul><li>
Waypoint locations
</li><li>
Flight altitude
</li><li>
Takeoff commands
</li><li>
Landing commands
</li><li>
Return-to-launch commands
</li><li>
Loiter commands
</li><li>
Camera operations
</li><li>
Survey or mapping patterns
</li><li>
Payload operations
</li><li>
Other automated actions
</li></ul>
<p>Before flying, the operator should carefully review the mission, verify appropriate altitudes and locations, check failsafe settings, and make sure the planned route can be flown safely.</p>
<h2 id="flight-modes">Flight modes</h2>
<p><strong>Flight modes</strong> determine how much control is provided by the pilot and how much assistance is provided by the flight controller.</p>
<h3 id="manual">Manual</h3>
<p>In <strong>Manual</strong> mode on a fixed-wing aircraft, pilot commands are generally passed directly to the aircraft&#x27;s control surfaces without attitude stabilization from the autopilot. The pilot is responsible for controlling the aircraft.</p>
<h3 id="fbwa-fly-by-wire-a">FBWA (Fly By Wire A)</h3>
<p>In <strong>FBWA</strong>, the pilot still flies the airplane, but the flight controller stabilizes it. Instead of directly commanding a control-surface position, the pilot commands a desired aircraft attitude within configured limits.</p>
<p>For example, moving the aileron stick commands the airplane to bank. When the pilot releases the stick, the controller commands the aircraft back toward level roll. Pitch is similarly constrained by configured limits.</p>
<p>FBWA therefore provides a useful combination of <strong>pilot control and automatic stabilization</strong>.</p>
<h3 id="fbwb-fly-by-wire-b">FBWB (Fly By Wire B)</h3>
<p><strong>FBWB</strong> adds additional assistance to FBWA. The flight controller manages aircraft behavior such as altitude and speed-related targets according to its configuration while the pilot continues to command the aircraft.</p>
<p>This provides more assistance than FBWA while still allowing the pilot to direct the airplane.</p>
<h3 id="auto">Auto</h3>
<p>In <strong>Auto</strong> mode, the flight controller follows a programmed mission. Depending on the aircraft and mission, the autopilot may control takeoff, navigation between waypoints, altitude, loitering, return-to-launch, and landing.</p>
<p>Even during an autonomous mission, the pilot must continue monitoring the aircraft and be prepared to intervene if necessary.</p>
<h2 id="using-mission-planner-to-fly-an-autonomous-mission">Using Mission Planner to fly an autonomous mission</h2>
<p>Teams that use Mission Planner can <a href="https://ardupilot.org/planner/">download it from ArduPilot</a>. Teams on QGroundControl can follow the <a href="#example-plan-a-mission-in-qgroundcontrol">QGroundControl mission example</a> earlier in this chapter.</p>
<p>Because flight-control software changes frequently, the <a href="/resources/mission-planning-lab/">mission planning lab</a> is intended to provide a <strong>general introduction to autonomous mission planning</strong> rather than step-by-step instructions for a particular software version.</p>
<p>For this activity, an aircraft will be programmed to take off, climb to a predetermined altitude, fly through several GPS waypoints, and return to the launch area. A fixed-wing airplane can then circle the launch location at a safe altitude until the pilot takes control and lands.</p>
